{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1110,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok, so the main video was talking about neutrinos\nshapeshifting via superposition, and a similar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5590,
    "dDurationMs": 3799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing happens if you take two pendulums and\nattach them with a spring – on their own,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9389,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they’ll want to do one of two things: either\nswing together in the same direction, or exactly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13999,
    "dDurationMs": 3801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "opposite each other, and when they’re opposite\neach other, they swing slightly faster because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17800,
    "dDurationMs": 1070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the help of the spring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18870,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But perhaps you interact with the pendulums,\nand push one of them to start it swinging."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22900,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When you think about it, the first pendulum\nswinging by itself is actually a superposition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27010,
    "dDurationMs": 5019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of both swinging the same way and both swinging\nopposite ways, combined so that the swinging"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32029,
    "dDurationMs": 2041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of pendulum two cancels out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34070,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, because the two “on their own”\nstates of the pendulums don’t swing with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38040,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same rate, they won’t cancel out for\npendulum two forever, and eventually it will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42200,
    "dDurationMs": 3410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be the only one swinging, and pendulum one\nwill have stopped."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45610,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then later, it’ll be pendulum one, and\nthen pendulum two, and so on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50290,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the states you interact with, just one\npendulum swinging, can oscillate back and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54570,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forth between each other, swapping identities,\nbecause they’re superpositions of the non-interacting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59840,
    "dDurationMs": 3050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "states, and those states have different frequencies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62890,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Other than a few pesky details to do with\nquantum mechanics, this is essentially what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66690,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happens with neutrinos!"
    } ]
  } ]
}
